---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Dynamic Modeling of Signaling Networks | Luis Firl"
  description="Ongoing research on data-driven inference and dynamic modeling of kinase signaling networks in triple-negative breast cancer."
>
  <section class="project-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ongoing Research · Systems Biology</p>

      <h1>
        Data-driven Dynamic Modeling of Signaling Networks in
        Triple-Negative Breast Cancer
      </h1>

      <p class="hero-text">
        A data-driven modeling framework for inferring kinase signaling
        dependencies from time-resolved phosphoproteomic data and integrating
        them into a mechanistic ODE model for dynamic simulation and
        perturbation analysis.
      </p>

      <div class="project-context">
        <div>
          <span class="context-label">Research environment</span>
          <p>
            Theoretical Biophysics · Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span class="context-label">Status</span>
          <p>Ongoing research</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="project-links">
        <a
          href="https://klipp-linding.science/index.php/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Research Group ↗
        </a>

        <a
          href="https://github.com/luisfirl/SignalingProject"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          GitHub ↗
        </a>

        <a
          href="/files/luis-firl-master-thesis.pdf"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
>
          Read Master's Thesis (PDF) ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="research-origin">
    <div class="container origin-grid">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research context</p>

      <div>
        <p class="large-text">
          The project originated from my Master's thesis in the Theoretical
          Biophysics group of Prof. Edda Klipp at Humboldt-Universität zu
          Berlin and is currently being further developed as part of an
          ongoing research collaboration.
        </p>

        <p>
          The broader goal is to develop quantitative models that connect
          experimentally observed phosphorylation dynamics with the underlying
          regulatory structure of cellular signaling networks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section border-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">From data to dynamics</p>

        <h2>
          Reconstructing dynamic signaling behavior from experimental
          measurements.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline" aria-label="Modeling workflow">
        <div>
          <span>Experimental data</span>
          <strong>Time-resolved phosphoproteomics</strong>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow">→</span>

        <div>
          <span>Inference</span>
          <strong>Network dependencies</strong>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow">→</span>

        <div>
          <span>Dynamics</span>
          <strong>Mechanistic ODE model</strong>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow">→</span>

        <div>
          <span>Analysis</span>
          <strong>In-silico perturbations</strong>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="two-column text-block">
        <h3>Why dynamic modeling?</h3>

        <div>
          <p>
            Cellular signaling pathways are not isolated linear cascades.
            Their behavior emerges from interacting kinases, feedback,
            crosstalk and time-dependent changes in phosphorylation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The modeling framework therefore aims to move beyond a static
            representation of signaling interactions and reconstruct how
            network activity evolves over time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Dependency inference</p>

        <h2>
          Estimating how signaling components influence one another.
        </h2>

        <p>
          A known network topology describes which interactions may occur,
          but not their relative strength. The model therefore introduces
          dependency parameters that are inferred directly from experimental
          phosphorylation trajectories.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="dependency-grid">
        <div class="parameter">
          <span class="symbol">α</span>

          <div>
            <h3>Kinase → phosphosite</h3>
            <p>
              Relative influence of an upstream kinase on the phosphorylation
              of a target site.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="parameter">
          <span class="symbol">β</span>

          <div>
            <h3>Phosphosite → kinase activity</h3>
            <p>
              Contribution of a kinase's phosphorylation state to its
              effective activity within the network.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="equation-block">
        <span class="equation-label">Dependency model</span>

        <p class="equation">
          Pᵢ(t) = αᵢ⁰ + Σⱼ αⱼⁱ ( βⱼ⁰ + Σₖ βⱼᵏ Pⱼᵏ(t) )
        </p>

        <p>
          The nested formulation links the phosphorylation state of upstream
          kinases to their influence on downstream target sites. Parameter
          estimation is performed under constraints that preserve the intended
          relative interpretation of these dependencies.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dynamics-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Mechanistic dynamics</p>

        <h2>
          From inferred dependencies to a coupled ODE network.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mechanism">
        <div>Transcription</div>
        <span>→</span>
        <div>mRNA</div>
        <span>→</span>
        <div>Protein</div>
        <span>⇄</span>
        <div>Phosphoprotein</div>
      </div>

      <div class="two-column text-block">
        <h3>Embedding network activity into kinetics</h3>

        <div>
          <p>
            The dynamic model describes transcription, translation,
            degradation, phosphorylation and dephosphorylation through a
            system of ordinary differential equations based on mass-action
            kinetics.
          </p>

          <p>
            The inferred signaling dependencies are embedded directly into
            the phosphorylation terms of the ODE system. In this way, the
            statistical information extracted from the experimental data
            becomes part of a mechanistic description of the network
            dynamics.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-flow">
        <span>Inferred dependencies</span>
        <strong>α, β</strong>
        <span>→</span>
        <span>Signaling activity</span>
        <span>→</span>
        <span>Phosphorylation kinetics</span>
        <span>→</span>
        <strong>ODE dynamics</strong>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Parameter inference</p>

        <h2>
          Fitting the data is only part of the problem.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Complementary numerical optimization strategies are used to estimate
          the constrained dependency parameters from experimental trajectories.
          Although different optimization formulations can reproduce the data
          similarly well, they do not necessarily identify the same underlying
          parameter configuration.
        </p>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        Good agreement with experimental observations does not necessarily
        imply unique identification of the underlying biological parameters.
      </blockquote>

      <p class="after-quote">
        This identifiability problem motivates the integration of additional
        biophysical information and constraints into future versions of the
        model.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section ongoing-section">
    <div class="container ongoing-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current work</p>
        <h2>The model is still evolving.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="ongoing-copy">
        <p>
          My current work focuses on improving the kinetic parameter
          estimation of the dynamic ODE model, with particular attention to
          numerical stability, computational efficiency and the quality of
          the resulting simulations.
        </p>

        <p>
          The modeling framework is intended to remain scalable so that the
          same underlying approach can be applied to increasingly large and
          interconnected signaling networks.
        </p>

        <p>
          In parallel, I am developing the scientific framework for a future
          publication together with collaborators involved in the experimental
          research underlying the project.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Toward predictive signaling models</p>

        <h2>
          From reconstruction toward prediction and perturbation.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="outlook-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Biophysical constraints</h3>
          <p>
            Integrating additional biological knowledge to improve parameter
            identifiability and mechanistic plausibility.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Improved inference</h3>
          <p>
            Developing more robust strategies for estimating kinetic and
            network parameters from experimental time-series data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Network perturbations</h3>
          <p>
            Using the dynamic model to investigate regulatory control,
            robustness and responses to targeted in-silico interventions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Scientific machine learning</h3>
          <p>
            Exploring how mechanistic models and modern learning approaches
            can be combined as larger experimental datasets become available.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project-hero {
    padding: 7rem 0 5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .project-hero h1 {
    max-width: 1050px;
    font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 6.5vw, 5.6rem);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .hero-text {
    max-width: 820px;
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.4rem);
  }

  .project-context {
    display: flex;
    gap: 5rem;
    margin-top: 4rem;
  }

  .context-label,
  .equation-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }

  .project-context p {
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
  }

  .project-links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .project-links a {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .research-origin {
    padding: 4rem 0;
    background: var(--surface);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .origin-grid,
  .ongoing-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 0.65fr 1.35fr;
    gap: 5rem;
  }

  .large-text {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2vw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .border-section {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .section-heading {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin-bottom: 3.5rem;
  }

  .section-heading.narrow {
    max-width: 800px;
  }

  .section-heading > p:last-child {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .pipeline {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 2rem 0;
    margin-bottom: 5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .pipeline div span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .pipeline strong {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .arrow {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .two-column {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 0.8fr 1.2fr;
    gap: 5rem;
  }

  .two-column h3 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }

  .two-column p:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .dependency-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    margin: 4rem 0;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .parameter {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 2.5rem;
  }

  .symbol {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: 3rem;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .parameter h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .equation-block {
    margin: 5rem 0 0;
    padding: 3rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .equation {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .equation-block > p:last-child {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .dynamics-section {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .mechanism {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 4rem 0 5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .mechanism div {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .mechanism span {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
  }

  .concept-flow {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    margin-top: 4rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .concept-flow strong {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  blockquote {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 4rem 0 2rem;
    padding-left: 2rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.3rem);
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .after-quote {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .ongoing-section {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .ongoing-copy {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
  }

  .ongoing-copy p:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .outlook-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .outlook-grid > div {
    padding: 2.5rem;
    background: var(--background);
  }

  .outlook-grid h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .project-hero {
      padding: 5rem 0 4rem;
    }

    .project-context,
    .project-links {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .origin-grid,
    .ongoing-grid,
    .two-column,
    .dependency-grid,
    .outlook-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
    }

    .pipeline {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .pipeline .arrow {
      transform: rotate(90deg);
      width: fit-content;
    }

    .mechanism {
      align-items: flex-start;
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .mechanism span {
      transform: rotate(90deg);
    }

    .equation-block {
      padding: 2rem 1.25rem;
    }
  }
</style>